body:

We ran both commands on Take 002, a three-hour Zoom F3 recording. The notes
below cover the input, the files written and two problems found during the run.

## Input and test conditions

We recorded Take 002 with two EM272 omnidirectional microphones connected to a
Zoom F3. The recorder wrote each microphone to its own synchronized mono file.
We passed those files to each command in track order, and both tools read them
in place.

- Files: `260115_002_Tr1.WAV` and `260115_002_Tr2.WAV`
- Recordist: Gewenxin Yu
- Microphones: two EM272 omnidirectional microphones
- Recorder: Zoom F3, firmware 1.10 metadata
- Encoding: 48 kHz, mono, 32-bit floating-point PCM per track
- Duration: 03:02:24 per track
- Input size: 2,101,379,072 bytes per track

## Making a small, self-describing excerpt by `soundcite`

We cut a 20-second passage beginning one hour into the take. Because we launched
the command from the recording directory, the manifest kept the source
filenames without exposing local absolute paths.

<pre class="software-example"><code>soundcite 260115_002_Tr1.WAV 260115_002_Tr2.WAV \
  --start 01:00:00 \
  --duration 20 \
  --title "Take 002 - 01:00:00 excerpt" \
  --recordist "Gewenxin Yu" \
  --recorded-at "2026-01-15T13:19:25+08:00" \
  --location "Yema Haizi, Kangding, Sichuan" \
  --license "CC BY-NC-SA 4.0" \
  --notes "Recorded with two EM272 omnidirectional microphones connected to a Zoom F3." \
  --output 260115-002-soundcite</code></pre>

- Runtime: about 2.2 seconds on the test workstation
- Selected range: 01:00:00.000–01:00:20.000
- Exact sample range: 172,800,000–173,760,000 at 48 kHz
- Track excerpts: two 32-bit float WAV files, 3,840,206 bytes each
- Preview: stereo Ogg Opus with an explicitly recorded peak limiter
- Other files: lossless-source spectrogram, manifest, `SHA256SUMS`, and static HTML

<div class="software-report__media">
  <audio controls preload="metadata">
    <source src="/files/software/260115-002-soundcite/preview.ogg" type="audio/ogg">
  </audio>
  <img
    src="/files/software/260115-002-soundcite/spectrogram.png"
    alt="Two-channel logarithmic spectrogram generated by soundcite">
  <p>
    Browser preview and two-channel logarithmic spectrogram generated in the
    same run. The preview is convenient but lossy; the spectrogram is generated
    from the two WAV excerpts, which preserve floating-point samples.
  </p>
</div>

### Files and provenance

- The two mono F3 files can be treated as one paired take without first creating
  a multi-gigabyte stereo file. Before extraction, the tool verifies their
  sample rate, duration, channel count, recording time, and BWF time reference.
- Embedded F3 fields such as scene, take, date, recording time, track name, and
  coding history survive into the manifest.
- `SHA256SUMS` covers both excerpts, the preview, spectrogram, manifest, and
  static page. We did not ask it to hash the complete source files in this run.
- The package labels the lossy listening copy separately from the
  preservation-oriented excerpts.

[Open the generated package](/files/software/260115-002-soundcite/index.html) | [Open the bare CLI package page (tool-generated HTML)](/files/software/260115-002-soundcite/index-bare.html) | [Read its manifest](/files/software/260115-002-soundcite/manifest.json) | [Verify its checksums](/files/software/260115-002-soundcite/SHA256SUMS)

## Screening the complete take in fixed windows by `lowdom`

`lowdom` decoded both tracks to mono at 4 kHz and divided them into
10-second windows. It measured those windows without writing a transformed
audio file.

<pre class="software-example"><code>lowdom 260115_002_Tr1.WAV 260115_002_Tr2.WAV \
  --window 10 \
  --threshold 0.95 \
  --output 260115-002-lowdom</code></pre>

- Runtime: about 67 seconds on the test workstation
- Analysed duration: 03:02:20
- Windows: 1,094 ten-second rows
- Default threshold: 0.95
- Rows above threshold: 833 (76.14%)
- Rows below threshold: 261 (23.86%)

### A provisional threshold from blind listening

The initial 0.6 threshold separated almost nothing. That value was only the
early CLI default—a placeholder not checked on this recording before the run.
We therefore tested 0.95 with two blinded listening rounds: each round contained ten randomly selected
windows above the threshold and ten below it. Of the 20 windows above 0.95, 19
were judged to contain obvious wind noise. Of the 20 below it, 17 were judged
not to contain obvious wind noise.

The threshold and listening judgement agreed on 36 of 40 samples. This is useful
for prioritising listening on this take, but it is not a validation study: the
sample is small, it comes from one recording setup, and it was reviewed by one
listener. The score remains a low-frequency screening heuristic rather than a
wind probability.

The source also lasts four seconds longer than the analysed duration. The
current code drops a final fragment shorter than half a window. We need to
report that fragment or make its omission an explicit option.

### Output we kept

- `windows.csv` retains both tracks' score, RMS level, low-frequency ratio,
  spectral flatness, and peak for every interval.
- `summary.json` records the frequency bands, sample rate, threshold, merge
  rule, source metadata, and totals.
- The static report allows a quick overview without Python, a notebook, or a
  server. The Mantle site report adds navigation and styling; the bare HTML 4.01
  page that `lowdom` writes to `report.html` is also kept in the package as
  `report-bare.html`.
- The multimedia report adds a lossy 48 kbit/s stereo preview. After enabling
  audio once, hovering over the timeline plays the corresponding ten seconds.
  The Mantle site report adds navigation and styling; the bare HTML 4.01 page
  the CLI writes as `report-multimedia.html` is also kept here as
  `report-multimedia-bare.html` (same `recording-preview-48k/` segments).
- No interval is silently removed from the source audio; the “candidate clean”
  file is only a list of timestamps.

[Open the multimedia report](/files/software/260115-002-lowdom/report-js.html) | [Open the bare CLI multimedia report](/files/software/260115-002-lowdom/report-multimedia-bare.html) | [Open the site data report](/files/software/260115-002-lowdom/report.html) | [Open the bare CLI timeline report](/files/software/260115-002-lowdom/report-bare.html) | [Download the window data](/files/software/260115-002-lowdom/windows.csv) | [Read the summary](/files/software/260115-002-lowdom/summary.json)

## Outcome of this test

`soundcite` did its job on this recording: it turned a timestamp into a small
package with enough information to trace the excerpt back to its sources.
`lowdom` completed the analysis, and the provisional 0.95 threshold produced
a useful listening split on this take.

Next, we need to report partial windows and repeat the listening test across
other recordings, microphones, wind protection and environments. “Low-frequency
screening” remains more accurate than “wind detection”.
